<p>In the latest escalation, Israeli strikes hit Iran’s South Pars gas field, and Iran retaliated by striking Qatar’s Ras Laffan energy complex, turning the Iran-Israel war into a direct threat to the region’s gas system. President Trump said the United States and Qatar were not involved in the South Pars strike, warned that Israel would not launch further attacks on the field, and threatened to destroy South Pars if Iran attacked Qatari facilities again. The attacks sent oil and gas prices sharply higher and deepened concern over shipping through the Strait of Hormuz, a corridor that handles about 20% of global oil and liquefied natural gas flows. At the same time, conflicting accounts emerged over whether Washington had prior knowledge of the South Pars attack. Trump said the U.S. “knew nothing” about that particular strike, while Israeli officials told several outlets that U.S. officials had been informed or had coordinated with Israel.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/HsovwpyQl-49E4PGQUcrP003khoLBwhNVuMe3_p1AC3oY7JbRZoG1c3wcPjs5aQr1TrB53uiQTzeVNKhxBkda80RgHl4ao16MbVJrZ4N7vobScycHDmT-ANrJx4zW5bkJTXWCEVhCLldcPiairiF-ZyWNkMJaFmP_Ye4PWRGffp5SSOoD56mJ-a5eyK4iS01t5fASv8S8SXFJPFJ2yn_U68W0BqKABdDgIIcroqpe859eqzIJHAIz9wq02I5uZhcrovTckMC5wKjJoa62w' alt='Qatar Energy said a missile hit its massive Ras Laffan LNG facility on Thursday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Price shock: Reuters reporting cited by Al Monitor said European gas prices surged 28% and oil gained 6%, while Semafor reported crude reaching $116 a barrel as markets priced in supply risk.</li><li>Targeting shift: Several reports described the South Pars strike as a major escalation because it hit a core gas-producing asset, and CBS cited an analyst saying the war is now hitting &quot;the plumbing of the global energy system&quot;.</li><li>Regional damage: Qatar said Iran’s strikes caused “extensive damage” at Ras Laffan, while Reuters reporting cited by Al Monitor said the UAE shut gas facilities and fires broke out at two Kuwaiti refineries.</li><li>Threat expansion: Iran’s Revolutionary Guards warned other Gulf Arab neighbors that their refineries and gas fields had become legitimate targets after the South Pars strike, raising the risk of further attacks beyond Iran and Qatar.</li><li>Iraq spillover: Iran halted gas flows to Iraq to redirect supplies at home after the South Pars strike, affecting a country that gets roughly one-third to 40% of its gas and power needs from Iran.</li></ul><blockquote>NO MORE ATTACKS WILL BE MADE BY ISRAEL - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Donald Trump: Trump said Israel acted alone in the South Pars strike, said the U.S. and Qatar were not involved, and framed his warning to Tehran as a deterrent against another attack on Qatar’s gas facilities. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/19/world/middleeast/trump-iran-south-pars-gas-field.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Israeli officials: Israeli officials told multiple outlets that Washington had prior knowledge of the South Pars strike, directly contradicting President Trump’s public account. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/19/world/middleeast/israel-iran-south-pars-gas-field-trump.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Qatar: Reporting summarized by The Week said Doha had no advance notice of the Israeli strike, and its condemnation of both sides reflected an effort to keep its LNG hub from becoming a battlefield in the wider war. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/recriminations-iran-war-gas-fields'>The Week</a>)</li><li>Iran: Iran’s leadership and military warned of broader retaliation if its energy infrastructure is hit again, with officials saying future responses would show “zero restraint.” (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-tehran-vows-zero-restraint-if-energy-sites-hit/live-76422645'>DW</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/19/att